      "id": "mrq4u36",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART A: WEATHERING"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Weathering is an in-situ (static) breakdown of rocks through mechanical, chemical, and biological processes.",
        "Erosion is a dynamic process involving the active removal and transportation of weathered rock material.",
        "Running water forms erosional features like V-shaped valleys and gorges, and depositional features like deltas and alluvial fans.",
        "Wind action in arid regions creates mushroom rocks and yardangs via erosion, and sand dunes and loess via deposition.",
        "Glaciers carve U-shaped valleys and cirques, while depositing unsorted sediments called moraines."
      ],
      "id": "ywcde51"
    },
